The bug report is unverified so far, but looking at the simplelru package on Github, I can see that the LRUCache struct has a doubly-linked list as its underlying EvictionCache. In the add method, it seems that the evicted entry is chosen by finding the least recently used entry (based on the time of the last access). This behavior is consistent with LRU caching algorithms. However, the report claims that the most recently used entry is being evicted instead.  

Let's take a look at how the doubly-linked list is updated on entry access. The most recently used entry's previous pointer is updated to reference the evicted entry. This is consistent with LRU algorithms, which generally try to keep the recently used entries closer to the front of the list. The problem might be in the way the LRU cache handles the eviction of entries, but based on the code, it seems to be doing what it's supposed to do.

To confirm, I need to look up the current implementation of the add method in the simplelru package and see how it updates the doubly-linked list. 

{"name": "read_file", "parameters": {"path":"github.com/hashicorp/golang-lru/v2/simplelru/lru.go"}}The bug report is accurate. The `removeOldest` function is incorrectly evicting the Most Recently Used (MRU) entry instead of the Least Recently Used (LRU) entry because it is calling `c.evictList.Front()` (which is the MRU) instead of `c.evictList.Back()` (which is the LRU).

I will fix this in `simplelru/lru.go` by updating `removeOldest` to use `c.evictList.Back()`.

I have fixed the issue in `simplelru/lru.go`. The `removeOldest` function incorrectly used `c.evictList.Front()` to get the oldest element, which is actually the most recently used (MRU) entry in the underlying linked list. I updated it to use `c.evictList.Back()`, which correctly retrieves the least recently used (LRU) entry, fulfilling the bug report's requirement.

